<b>ו.</b> The binding case, etc. To remember before Hashem, blessed be He, the right of fathers and to remember that he must surrender his soul to Hashem, blessed be He, and the passage of the manna, that he may believe that all his sustenance is in nothing from the providence of Hashem, blessed be He. He wrote in the Sefer HaKonavat Dish for the Omru before the Torah of the Sacrifices and there is for the Omru even on Shabbat. He wrote in the 19th century, page Rabb, that he should not say on Shabbat and Yom Tov in sacrifices, but always a sacrifice and all that accompanies it, and muspin and incense, because they are sacrificed on Shabbat and Yom Tov, but the rest of the whole portion of the sacrifices is not to be said at all, even if his intention is as a Torah reader, up to this point, and in Eliyahu Zota I wrote in the name of Lechem Hamudot, page 25, until he who has a heart to understand and learn is good to learn, and it is enough for him to do the prescribed prayers and instead of supplications and sacrifices He will study up to this point, and in short, he brought my words and the khashas, the time of the Torah for one time, the time of prayer for one, and not for a minute, the eighteen prayers and the reading of the Shema Kai, as well as Kasha Desuthar himself, in the laws of the laying on of hands, ruled according to my words. I also found this in the passages of Thos A person who is not busy with his business and is not a Torah scholar does not have to prevent him from saying every day the words of prayer and other verses printed in siddurs, but a person who is busy with his business has to refrain from saying them, and also a Torah scholar it is good for him to study in their place, so far. They also wrote in the verses of Thos. It is forbidden to say prayers and supplications in the verses of Dezmera or when the cantor repeats the prayer, so far. The grammarians also wrote that if he doesn't say mamutos every day, he should say in any case at dawn immediately after Parshat HaTamid a psalm of a day's song and even on Shabbat he should say a psalm of a song for the Shabbat day and on Shabbat. And then they will say, "You are the one who burned the incense, etc. And Hashem said to Moses, etc., to fatten the incense, as it is printed in the Ma'amat, and then it is not necessary to say in the addition, even on Shabbat and Yom Tov, so far." And I also saw in the 16th century the sign of Kalab to say before the prayer, except that the tadak deprashat of the tamidim is against the blood of the tamidim and the prayer is against the organs of the tamidim. No, why didn't you let me, and that's why he wrote to say the incense after the tamidim so that he reads between the blood and the organs. And I wonder if this grammar is a rule of thumb at the time when the constant sacrifice was actually offered, they would pray afterwards in the sukkah, page 13, and more in our words Yona Rish, the chapter of the morning prayer is explained in the main prayers against the blood. And in any case, Dina is right. And not on behalf of, but he found it so in the answer of Menachem Azaria, see 14, see the name of his taste. Written in the order of the day, I saw a custom that says in the interpretation of the song that the Levites used to say in the temple on Sunday a psalm to David, etc. But in Menachem Azariah's answer to Siman 25 it is explained that one should not say yes. And look at the states of these songs. The author of the Ora Parshath and the Athanan Ha Dayin say a supplication on Shabbat and a good day.<br>
<br>
<b>ז.</b> The Ten mitzvot, etc. In Eliyahu Zota I wrote, the Rashel used to say them first Baruch he said because it has ten times Baruch until Baruch you Hashem, and his zekino (Zakino's interpretation of the Rashel and Gan Neta was wrong in my words, see ibid) he used to say the first Parashat Anchi in the morning and the second in the Parashat and I beg you in the evening another who said the Shema reading on his mat up to this point. The students of the late Ari wrote that he would say every day four remembrances of the giving of the Torah and the revival of Amalek and the remembrance of Miriam who became a leper. He wrote in the 5th page of the 28th chapter of what is found in our siddurim, the request arranged according to verses, which is signed with the blessing Baruch thou, O Hashem, who hears a prayer, and also signs some other supplications and requests that are printed in other places. It is a mistake, because this blessing was not mentioned in the Shas and the rulings, and the blessing is a blessing for a nullity, but this is what it would say: Baruch, thou hearest a prayer. Without mentioning the name, so far, and refer to Lakman sign MU, section 7 and 17 at the end of sign 11:<br>
